The bound is trivial if one of $\eps$, $\delta$, or $d/q$ is at least~$1$, so assume all three are at most~$1$. In Lean, the third assumption is recorded as $d \le q$, which is equivalent to $d/q \le 1$ since $q>0$. Apply Lemma~ef{lem:self-improvement-helper} to~$G$ and let $\widehat H \in \polysub{m}{q}{d}$ be the output, with \[ \widehat{\zeta} = 100m \cdot \Big(\eps^{1/2} + \delta^{1/2} + (d/q)^{1/2}\Big). \] By ef{item:self-improvement-self} of Lemma~ef{lem:self-improvement-helper}, \[ \sum_{h}  ra{\psi} \widehat{H}_h \ot \widehat{H}_h \ket{\psi} \geq  ra{\psi} \widehat{H} \ot I \ket{\psi} - \widehat{\zeta}. \] Therefore Theorem~ef{thm:orthonormalization} supplies a projective submeasurement $H \in \polysub{m}{q}{d}$ such that  egin{equation} \widehat{H}_h \ot I \approx_{\widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{ortho}}} H_{h} \ot I, \end{equation} where \[ \widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{ortho}} = 100 \widehat{\zeta}^{1/4}. \] In addition, Lemma~ef{prop:self-consistency-implies-data-processing} implies that  egin{equation} \widehat{H}_{[h(u)=a]} \ot I \approx_{\widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{dataprocess}}} H_{[h(u)=a]} \ot I, \end{equation} where \[ \widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{dataprocess}} = 8 \widehat{\zeta} + 8 \sqrt{\widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{ortho}}}. \] The four conclusions are transferred one by one. \emph{Proof of ef{item:self-improvement-projective-completeness}.} Lemma~ef{prop:completeness-transfer-self-consistent-A} implies \[  ra{\psi} H \ot I \ket{\psi} \geq  ra{\psi} \widehat{H} \ot I \ket{\psi} - \widehat{\zeta} - 2\sqrt{\widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{ortho}}} \geq (1-
u) - 2\widehat{\zeta} - 2\sqrt{\widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{ortho}}}, \] where the second inequality is ef{item:self-improvement-completeness} of Lemma~ef{lem:self-improvement-helper}. \emph{Proof of ef{item:self-improvement-projective-A-consistency}.} Lemma~ef{prop:triangle-sub} applied to ef{item:self-improvement-A-consistency} of Lemma~ef{lem:self-improvement-helper} and the post-processed comparison~\eqref{eq:approx-data-processed} gives \[ A^u_a \ot I \simeq_{\widehat{\zeta} + \sqrt{\widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{dataprocess}}}} I \ot H_{[h(u) = a]}. \] In Lean the corresponding transport is stated for submeasurements with the total-overlap displacement recorded explicitly; this is the term which vanishes in the measurement-valued form of Lemma~ef{prop:triangle-sub}. The monotone-total route records a sufficient structural replacement: if the orthonormalization repair preserves the completed residual outcome, then the projective total is bounded by the helper total as an operator, hence also has no larger right-register expectation in the strategy state. Under this structural hypothesis the alphabet-size displacement term is not introduced. The QXP-layer interface used by the formalization records both the total domination of the canonical projective family and the sharper residual comparison for the fresh option-completion outcome. At present the formal QXP algebra only isolates the construction-level statement \(Q_{\mathrm{none}} \leq P_{\mathrm{none}}\), obtained when the repair preserves the fresh row block \(\widehat X_{\mathrm{none}}=X_{\mathrm{none}}\). The former generic ``RestrictSome'' monotone-total route would have required one to turn this into the source-facing inequality \(\widehat A_{ ot} \leq \widehat P_{ ot}\), by adding a comparison between the completed source residual and the ``Q''-layer fresh outcome. The present formal development no longer uses that generic route, and the comparison is false without additional hypotheses; see 	exttt{docs/reports/issue-1642-restrictsome-residual-domination-obstruction.md}. Since \(A^u_{\mathrm{tot}}=I\) and postprocessing does not change the total operator, Lean also records the equivalent form in which this displacement is the single scalar difference \[ \left| \langle \psi, I \ot H_{\mathrm{tot}} \psiangle - \langle \psi, I \ot \widehat H_{\mathrm{tot}} \psiangle ight| \] between the right-register totals of \(H\) and \(\widehat H\). The outcomewise data-processing bound controls this scalar by applying the vector triangle inequality to the sum over \(a\in\F_q\); the formal estimate gives the additional term \[ \sqrt{q\,\widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{dataprocess}}}. \] Thus the total-overlap term is no longer an independent hypothesis, although the numerical absorption of this alphabet-size loss is recorded separately. There is also a sharper formal route: if the right-register total of the projective family has no larger expectation than that of \(\widehat H\), then the total-overlap term can only improve the consistency defect. Under this monotonicity hypothesis the transported error is again \(\widehat{\zeta}+\sqrt{\widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{dataprocess}}}\), and the standard final-stage numerical estimate absorbs it into \(\zeta\). \emph{Proof of ef{item:self-improvement-projective-self}.} Lemma~ef{prop:two-notions-of-self-consistency} implies \[ \widehat{H}_h \ot I \approx_{2\widehat{\zeta}} I \ot \widehat{H}_h. \] Combining this with~\eqref{eq:approx-between-H-with-and-without-hat} on both sides and summing the three transports with Lemma~ef{prop:triangle-inequality-for-approx_delta} yields \[ H_h \ot I \approx_{6 \widehat{\zeta} + 6\widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{ortho}}} I \ot H_h. \] \emph{Proof of ef{item:self-improvement-projective-boundedness}.} The boundedness quantity is  egin{align} & ra{\psi} Z \ot (I - H) \ket{\psi} 
otag\ ={}&  ra{\psi} Z \ot I \ket{\psi} - \sum_h  ra{\psi} Z \ot H_h \ket{\psi} 
otag\ \leq{}&  ra{\psi} Z \ot I \ket{\psi} - \sum_h  ra{\psi} \E_{u} A^{u}_{h(u)} \ot H_h \ket{\psi} 
otag\ ={}&  ra{\psi} Z \ot I \ket{\psi} -\E_{u} \sum_h  ra{\psi} A^{u}_{h(u)} \ot H_h \ket{\psi} 
otag\ ={}&  ra{\psi} Z \ot I \ket{\psi} -\E_{u} \sum_a  ra{\psi} A^{u}_{a} \ot H_{[h(u)=a]} \ket{\psi}. \end{align} Proposition~ef{prop:easy-approx-from-approx-delta} upgrades \eqref{eq:approx-data-processed} to the scalar estimate \[ \eqref{eq:projective-boundedness-expanded} \le  ra{\psi} Z \ot I \ket{\psi} -\E_{u} \sum_a  ra{\psi} A^{u}_{a} \ot \widehat H_{[h(u)=a]} \ket{\psi} + \sqrt{\widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{dataprocess}}}, \] and now ef{item:self-improvement-boundedness} of Lemma~ef{lem:self-improvement-helper} bounds this by $\widehat{\zeta} + \sqrt{\widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{dataprocess}}}$. The formal boundedness field is the corresponding operator-monotonicity step: once the SDP dual witness satisfies $I\le Z$, the total mass of any left-placed submeasurement, and in particular of the final projective submeasurement, is bounded by $Z\ot I$ with zero boundedness error. Thus the four conclusions hold with errors \[ 2\widehat{\zeta} + 2\sqrt{\widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{ortho}}}, \qquad \widehat{\zeta} + \sqrt{\widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{dataprocess}}}, \qquad 6 \widehat{\zeta} + 6\widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{ortho}}, \qquad \widehat{\zeta} + \sqrt{\widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{dataprocess}}}, \] respectively. The paper's exponent bookkeeping then shows the following bounds. The formal bookkeeping uses the three-term power sum $\eps^p + \delta^p + (d/q)^p$, its monotonicity on the unit interval, and the corresponding square-root estimate. The displayed Lean theorems record these arithmetic identities together with the completeness, self-closeness, and projective-residual absorptions $2 \widehat{\zeta} + 2\sqrt{\widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{ortho}}} \le \zeta$, $6 \widehat{\zeta} + 6\widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{ortho}} \le \zeta$ and $\widehat{\zeta} + \sqrt{\widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{dataprocess}}} \le \zeta$. The two threshold lemmas involving \(|\mathbb F_q|\) record the scalar part of the alphabet-size obstruction in the current final-fields point-consistency transport: the data-processing threshold contains \(8\widehat{\zeta}\), but the transported estimate still carries the factor \(\sqrt{|\mathbb F_q|}\). \[ \widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{ortho}} \leq 400 m \cdot \Big(\eps^{1/8} + \delta^{1/8} + (d/q)^{1/8}\Big), \] \[ \widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{dataprocess}} \leq 960m \cdot \Big(\eps^{1/16} + \delta^{1/16} + (d/q)^{1/16}\Big), \] and hence \[ 6 \widehat{\zeta} + 6\widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{ortho}} \le \zeta, \qquad \widehat{\zeta} + \sqrt{\widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{dataprocess}}} \le \zeta. \] Since $2\widehat{\zeta} + 2\sqrt{\widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{ortho}}} \le \widehat{\zeta}_{\mathrm{dataprocess}} \le \zeta$, all four bounds are at most~$\zeta$.

